\details{
\strong{The date grids.} "Cases per event date" is a statement about a \emph{calendar},
not about the rows present in the data, so each axis is completed to a full
grid running from the earliest observed date on that axis to \code{\link[=get_now]{get_now()}},
stepping by that axis's units. Dates with no rows count as zeros. This is
what makes \code{prop_zero} and the zero-run lengths meaningful, and it is why a
line list -- which cannot represent a zero -- is summarised correctly here.

\strong{Not-yet-observed cells are dropped.} An \code{NA} count means the cell has not
been observed yet, unlike a \code{0}, which was observed and was zero. Such rows
carry no cases, so they are excluded rather than allowed to turn every total
they touch into \code{NA}. How many were dropped is reported as the
\code{"unobserved_cells"} coverage row.

The grid is \strong{global}: when \code{by_strata = TRUE} every stratum is summarised
on the same grid, so a stratum whose cases start late genuinely shows the
leading zeros. Otherwise the strata would not be comparable.

\strong{Count-cumulative data gets no delay rows.} A cumulative total is not
additive across delays, so a case-weighted delay distribution would be
meaningless. The \code{"growth"} rows take their place, describing how each event
date's total grows from one delay to the next. Call
\code{to_count(x, to = "count-incidence")} first if you want the delay
distribution -- and note that de-accumulating can produce negative
increments.
